(• 1. Commands vs. Expressions: CPL was the first major language to make a strict theoretical distinction between "Commands" \(statements) Tj
T* (executed solely for their side effects, such as mutating memory\) and "Expressions" \(mathematical constructs evaluated strictly to produce a value) Tj
T* (without side effects\).) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 8 Tf
9.1999999999999993 TL
0.2 0.255 0.333 rg
39.6850393700787407 341.2915748031495582 Td
(• 2. Polymorphic and Abstract Types: CPL featured an advanced type system supporting integers, reals, booleans, labels, pointers, and) Tj
T* (user-defined records, alongside polymorphic functions that could operate on multiple data types.) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 8 Tf
9.1999999999999993 TL
0.2 0.255 0.333 rg
39.6850393700787407 315.4962992125983874 Td
(• 3. First-Class Functions: Functions in CPL were first-class citizens: they could be passed as arguments to other routines, returned from) Tj
T* (expressions, and assigned to variables.) Tj
ET
