(• Stripping Down BCPL: Thompson distilled BCPL even further, discarding everything that could not fit into the PDP-7’s memory, creating the) Tj
T* (language simply called "B" \(named either after BCPL or Thompson’s wife, Bonnie\).) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 8 Tf
9.1999999999999993 TL
0.2 0.255 0.333 rg
39.6850393700787407 496.9136220472440755 Td
(• The B Syntax: Thompson introduced the concise operators that programmers use today: the `++` and `--` increment/decrement operators \(which) Tj
T* (matched the PDP-7 hardware auto-increment address registers\), composite assignment operators \(`+=`, `-=`\), and minimalist keywords.) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 8 Tf
9.1999999999999993 TL
0.2 0.255 0.333 rg
39.6850393700787407 471.1183464566929047 Td
(• Word-Based Memory in B: Like BCPL, B was completely typeless. Every variable occupied exactly one 16-bit PDP-7 memory word. Memory) Tj
T* (pointers were word addresses rather than byte addresses.) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 8 Tf
9.1999999999999993 TL
0.2 0.255 0.333 rg
39.6850393700787407 445.3230708661417339 Td
(• The Fatal Limitation of B: When Bell Labs acquired a DEC PDP-11 in 1970, the hardware supported 8-bit byte addressing. B’s untyped) Tj
T* (word-based model struggled to handle 8-bit ASCII characters without expensive bit-shifting, exposing the critical need for data types.) Tj
ET
