(1. Memory Vulnerability Hazards: \(a\) If caller fails to delete[] the pointer, a heap memory leak occurs; \(b\) If caller deletes the) Tj
T* (pointer twice, it triggers a double-free heap metadata corruption crash; \(c\) Any access to the pointer after deletion constitutes) Tj
T* (undefined behavior via a dangling pointer.) Tj
T* (2. Double-Free Corruption: Heap allocators maintain linked lists of free memory chunks \(bins\). Freeing an already freed chunk) Tj
T* (corrupts the allocator’s internal linked list pointers, allowing arbitrary memory write exploits.) Tj
T* (3. Modern C++ RAII Solution: Replace raw pointer with std::vector<int> or std::unique_ptr<int[]>, which guarantees) Tj
T* (deterministic automatic deallocation upon scope exit without manual delete calls.) Tj
ET
